---
import CourseSlideLayout from '../../../layouts/CourseSlideLayout.astro';
import TirfAiRoles from '../../../components/lessons/TirfAiRoles.astro';
import TirfStrategyMatrix from '../../../components/lessons/TirfStrategyMatrix.astro';
import TirfFramework from '../../../components/lessons/TirfFramework.astro';
import LessonDeckChrome from '../../../components/lessons/LessonDeckChrome.astro';
import { tirfReferences } from '../../../data/tirfSources';
import { workshopTitle, agenda, prompts, worksheet, cases } from '../../../data/tirfWorkshop';
import '../../../styles/tirf-workshop.css';
const title = `${workshopTitle} · April Jiawei Zhang`;
const description = 'A hands-on AI4ELE / TIRF workshop: use Codex and WorkBuddy to design classroom language-assessment activities using source-supported formative-assessment strategies and a goal-centred model.';
const courseHref = '/teaching/training/';
const resources = '/teaching/training/tirf/resources/';
---
<CourseSlideLayout {title} {description} lessonDeck>
<section class="tirf-access" data-tirf-access aria-labelledby="tirf-access-title">
<p>AI4ELE / TIRF · 24 October 2026</p><h1 id="tirf-access-title">{workshopTitle}</h1><p>April Jiawei Zhang · City University of Macau</p>
<form data-tirf-access-form><label for="tirf-access-code">Workshop access code</label><div><input id="tirf-access-code" inputmode="numeric" autocomplete="off" required aria-describedby="tirf-access-feedback" /><button type="submit">Open workshop</button></div><p id="tirf-access-feedback" role="status"></p></form><a href={courseHref}>Back to training</a><noscript>Enable JavaScript to use this interactive workshop.</noscript>
</section>
<div data-tirf-workshop hidden>
<LessonDeckChrome {courseHref} courseLabel="training materials" lessonTitle="AI empowered formative assessment" />
<main class="deck tirf-deck" aria-label="AI empowered formative assessment workshop">
<section class="slide is-active" data-title="Welcome" aria-hidden="false"><div class="slide-inner title-slide">
<header class="slide-header"><span class="eyebrow">AI4ELE / TIRF · BNBU, Zhuhai · 24 October 2026</span></header><div class="title-rule"></div>
<div data-language="en"><p class="source-note">Submitted workshop title</p><h1 class="cover-title">AI Empowered Formative Assessment in TESOL: <span>Designing Feedback Reflection and Learner Action</span></h1><p class="lead">1. Understand what makes assessment formative.<br />2. Use Codex or WorkBuddy to design for formative purposes in language education.</p><p>Dr. April Jiawei Zhang 張家維博士<br />School of Education, City University of Macau<br />15:10–16:10 · T6-705</p></div>
<div data-language="zh" lang="zh-Hant" hidden><p class="source-note">原提交工作坊題目</p><h1 class="cover-title">AI賦能TESOL形成性評估<span>設計回饋、反思與學習者行動</span></h1><p class="lead">一、理解甚麼使評估具有形成性。<br />二、運用Codex或WorkBuddy設計促進語言學習的活動。</p><p>張家維博士 · 澳門城市大學教育學院<br />2026年10月24日 · 15:10–16:10 · T6-705</p></div>
<p class="source-note">Our design guides: <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s11092-008-9068-5">Black &amp; Wiliam’s (2009) five strategies</a> and the supplied <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.system.2024.103414">goal-centred purpose–practice–effect model (Li &amp; Gu, 2024)</a>.</p>
<div class="actions"><button data-jump="1">Agenda and preparation</button><button class="secondary" data-jump="4">Frameworks guiding our design</button><button class="secondary" data-reading aria-pressed="false">Switch to reading view</button></div><p class="source-note">Use the slide counter for section navigation. Full screen is optional. Your workshop notes stay in this open page until you export them.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="slide" data-title="Agenda and preparation" aria-hidden="true"><div class="slide-inner">
<header class="slide-header"><span class="eyebrow">Before the hour begins</span></header>
<div data-language="en"><h2>A working hour, in pairs</h2><p>One person builds; one checks the learning design. Swap roles for peer testing.</p></div><div data-language="zh" lang="zh-Hant" hidden><h2>兩人協作的一小時</h2><p>一人操作，一人檢視教學設計；同伴測試時交換角色。</p></div>
<ol class="agenda">{agenda.map(([time,en,zh,index])=><li><button data-jump={index}><time>{time} min</time><span data-language="en">{en}</span><span data-language="zh" lang="zh-Hant" hidden>{zh}</span></button></li>)}</ol>
<details><summary>Before arrival: install, sign in, try a tiny HTML / 課前準備</summary><p>Download <a href="https://www.workbuddy.cn/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tencent WorkBuddy</a>, install and sign in, then ask it to make a tiny HTML page in a new empty folder. Open the result. The official guide documents Windows 10+ (x64), macOS 12+, and personal sign-in with WeChat, phone or email.</p><p>Generation needs internet. A completed dependency-free activity can run offline. Usage limits and plans may change; no paid subscription is compulsory for this workshop. Pair up or use our prepared HTML/paper fallback if installation, connectivity or usage allowance gets in the way.</p><p>Facilitator: Codex. Participants: WorkBuddy or paired work. The exact buttons may differ; practise the workflow. Use fictional text, a single isolated folder and normal permission checks. Do not give full-access permissions or put real learner data into prompts.</p><p><a href="https://cloud.tencent.com/document/product/1831/134387">Installation</a> · <a href="https://cloud.tencent.com/document/product/1831/134389">Getting started</a> · <a href="https://cloud.tencent.com/document/product/1831/134400">Files and preview</a></p></details>
</div></section>
<section class="slide" data-title="0–6 · Informing or forming?" aria-hidden="true"><div class="slide-inner">
<header class="slide-header"><span class="eyebrow">0–6 min · Part 1: Understanding formative assessment</span></header>
<div data-language="en"><h2>What would you teach next?</h2><p>Both activities ask learners to choose the better-supported paragraph. Which evidence lets you make a better-founded decision?</p></div><div data-language="zh" lang="zh-Hant" hidden><h2>接下來教甚麼？</h2><p>兩個活動都要求選出論據較充分的段落。哪一種證據更能支持下一步教學？</p></div>
<div class="two"><article><h3>Marks-only quiz / 只有分數</h3><p>“B. Correct. 1/1.”</p><p>You know the selection. What does this leave uncertain?</p></article><article><h3>Evidence for action / 支持行動的證據</h3><p>“B, because it is longer.”</p><p>The selection is the same. Would your next step be the same?</p></article></div>
<label for="opening-notice">Discuss: what do you still need to find out? / 還需了解甚麼？</label><textarea id="opening-notice" data-note="Opening observation" rows="2"></textarea>
<details><summary>After discussing: reveal one teaching possibility / 討論後展開</summary><p>Try two equally long examples, one relevant and one irrelevant. Ask the learner to justify a choice, then ask for a fresh justification to check whether the learner can explain relevance. “Correct” alone cannot show why B was chosen.</p><p>We noticed a length-based reason. We will investigate relevance. We will check again by asking for a fresh justification.</p></details><p class="takeaway">Information about performance informs. It becomes formative use when evidence-informed teacher action is intended to close the identified learning gap, with learner take-up and a way to check the effect. Monitoring or displaying feedback alone does not demonstrate development.</p>
<details><summary>Contrast: weekly reflective blogs / 每周反思博客</summary><div class="two"><article><h3>Informing only: non-formative as presented</h3><p>Students write about what was taught and what still puzzles them, responding to topic prompts. The teacher tracks understanding, ongoing language use and engagement.</p><p>The original example shows information and monitoring. No purposeful developmental follow-up or check of progress is shown. The word “reflection” does not establish formative use.</p></article><article><h3>Our proposed redesign</h3><p>Check a recurring difficulty, investigate its likely cause, arrange focused support intended to close that gap, and ask learners to apply a strategy in a new task. Inspect fresh evidence to check the effect.</p><p>This is a plan intended to support development, not a claim that development has already occurred.</p></article></div><p class="source-note">Weekly reflective-blog example supplied for discussion; no citation was visible. The redesign is proposed here and is not part of the original example.</p></details>
<details><summary>Contrast: dictation, information and development / 聽寫</summary><ol><li>Dictation is given.</li><li>Students mark their work and highlight the correct form from a master.</li><li>Teacher and learners receive information about spelling, listening and grammar.</li><li>Information is used to modify instruction or guide self-study.</li></ol><p>Steps 1–3 inform. Step 4 is formative only if the action is directed at closing an identified gap. “Modify instruction” alone is too vague. What will the teacher and learner actually do, and how will they check its effect?</p><p class="source-note">Original sequence from the teaching example supplied for discussion; the conditional interpretation follows the workshop distinction. No developmental result is claimed.</p></details><p class="source-note">Black &amp; Wiliam (2009)</p>
</div></section>
<section class="slide" data-title="6–12 · Language aims from learner needs" aria-hidden="true"><div class="slide-inner">
<header class="slide-header"><span class="eyebrow">6–12 min · What language learning needs attention?</span></header>
<div data-language="en"><h2>What are these learners trying to get better at?</h2><p>What am I assessing when I assess my students formatively in class? Begin with learner evidence and needs, rather than copying a textbook objective.</p><p class="lead">Support an opinion with a specific relevant example and explain its link.</p></div><div data-language="zh" lang="zh-Hant" hidden><h2>一個目標，兩項可觀察準則</h2><p class="lead">以具體相關的例子支持觀點，並解釋兩者的聯繫。</p></div>
<div class="two"><article><h3>Criterion 1 / 準則一</h3><p>A specific example is relevant to the claim.</p></article><article><h3>Criterion 2 / 準則二</h3><p>An explanation shows how the example supports the claim.</p></article></div>
<details><summary>Critique a textbook activity list / 檢視課本目標</summary><p><strong>Are these useful targets for formative assessment in a language class?</strong></p><ol><li>Read about the virtual choir.</li><li>Listen to people talk about their music preferences, and share your own.</li><li>Write a speech about how music has helped you or someone you know.</li><li>Learn about musical scores in films.</li></ol><p>The theme offers useful context. Items 2 and 3 involve language activities, but the list does not yet specify which aspect of language use these learners need to improve.</p><label for="target-rewrite">Rewrite one item around a language need / 按語言學習需要改寫</label><textarea id="target-rewrite" data-note="Learner-specific aim rewrite" rows="2"></textarea><p><strong>Conditional workshop adaptation:</strong> if learners state a music preference but cannot explain it, focus on explaining a preference with a relevant reason and concrete example. What evidence would show that language improvement, and what support is needed?</p><p class="source-note">Textbook example supplied for critique; its identity and edition were not visible. This conditional rewrite is our workshop adaptation, not an objective attributed to that textbook.</p></details>
<details><summary>Check the interpretation before choosing support / 先核對詮釋</summary><p>A dictation error could reflect sound perception, spelling, grammar, vocabulary or attention. Use a short diagnostic follow-up to distinguish plausible explanations before deciding support.</p></details>
<p class="takeaway">The topic is a vehicle. Choose an actionable focus from what these learners need to develop; use evidence to make that choice.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="slide" data-title="12–18 · Five strategies and goal-centred practice" aria-hidden="true"><div class="slide-inner"><header class="slide-header"><span class="eyebrow">12–18 min · Part 1: Purpose, practice and effect</span></header><div data-language="en"><h2>Use two complementary guides</h2><p>Use the five strategies to guide design decisions. Then explore how the goal-centred model connects the practices in our language task. A sound process still needs its effects checked.</p></div><div data-language="zh" lang="zh-Hant" hidden><h2>運用兩個互補的指引</h2><p>以五項策略指導設計，再探索以目標為中心的實踐模型。即使過程合理，仍需檢視實際發展。</p></div><TirfStrategyMatrix />
<p class="source-note">Next, keep the language-learning target central while connecting evidence, interpretation, feedback and purposeful action.</p>
<TirfFramework />

<details><summary>When do we stop, investigate or support? / 何時繼續、診斷或支援？</summary><div class="stopping-grid"><article><h3>Goal met</h3><p>Move on, extend or confirm the planned next step. Do not force another feedback cycle.</p></article><article><h3>Evidence unclear</h3><p>Check instructions and seek diagnostic evidence before drawing a conclusion.</p></article><article><h3>Gap evident</h3><p>Offer focused feedback, reteach or targeted support, then recheck when useful, including in a later task.</p></article></div></details>
<p class="takeaway">A polished interface cannot repair a goal–task mismatch. Agree what counts as evidence before asking AI to code.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="slide" data-title="18–26 · Codex build–test–improve" aria-hidden="true"><div class="slide-inner">
<header class="slide-header"><span class="eyebrow">18–26 min · Part 2: Designing with tools</span></header>
<div data-language="en"><h2>Artificial intelligence for formative assessment: potentials and boundaries</h2><p>GenAI systems create text, images, audio and other content in response to prompts; coding tools can generate an activity. Formative use depends on teachers and learners interpreting evidence and acting to support development.</p></div><div data-language="zh" lang="zh-Hant" hidden><h2>觀察決策，再親身試用</h2></div>
<TirfAiRoles /><ol class="workshop-steps"><li><strong>Build · 3 min</strong><span>Open an isolated folder. Review the brief. Generate one index.html.</span></li><li><strong>Test · 3 min</strong><span>Open in a browser. Try blank and weak responses. Inspect actual text export.</span></li><li><strong>Improve · 2 min</strong><span>Make one evidence-led repair. Reopen and recheck it.</span></li></ol>
<p>Use the lab on the next section as the prepared fallback if generation is slow. Ask: “Which design decision makes this formative?”</p>
{prompts.slice(0,2).map((p,i)=><details><summary>{i+1}. {p.title}</summary><pre id={`prompt-${i}`}>{p.text}</pre><button data-copy={`prompt-${i}`}>Copy prompt {i+1}</button><p class="status" data-copy-status={i} role="status"></p></details>)}
<p class="source-note">The coding assistant builds the activity. The finished classroom tool uses teacher-approved local hints. No runtime LLM, key, database, recording or learner login is needed.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="slide" data-title="18–26 · Try the learner lab" aria-hidden="true"><div class="slide-inner">
<header class="slide-header"><span class="eyebrow">18–26 min · Learner-facing demonstration</span></header>
<div data-language="en"><h2>Try the Evidence and Explanation Lab</h2><p>Choose and justify, locate the example and link, improve A, then use hints to revise. Finally explain what changed and why.</p></div><div data-language="zh" lang="zh-Hant" hidden><h2>試用「例證與解釋實驗室」</h2><p>選擇並說明理由，找出例子與聯繫，改寫A，依提示修訂，最後解釋改動及原因。活動以英語進行。</p></div>
<div class="actions"><a class="download" href={`${resources}evidence-lab.html`} download>Download offline lab</a><a href={`${resources}evidence-lab.html`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open lab in its own tab</a></div><p class="source-note">Intermediate English learners; not a formally validated CEFR assessment. No multi-device response collection. Print/export within the lab to keep your own writing.</p>
<iframe title="Evidence and Explanation Lab interactive activity" src={`${resources}evidence-lab.html`} sandbox="allow-scripts allow-downloads allow-modals" loading="lazy"></iframe>
</div></section>
<section class="slide" data-title="26–29 · Pair design brief" aria-hidden="true"><div class="slide-inner">
<header class="slide-header"><span class="eyebrow">26–29 min · Plan in pairs</span></header>
<div data-language="en"><h2>Design a small activity for your classroom</h2><p>Keep the goal narrow enough to test in a few minutes. Use fictional responses and agree the two criteria with your partner.</p></div><div data-language="zh" lang="zh-Hant" hidden><h2>為自己的課堂設計一個小活動</h2><p>目標需能在數分鐘內檢視。使用虛構作答，與同伴商定兩項準則。</p></div>
<div class="worksheet">{worksheet.map(([id,label,placeholder])=><label for={`brief-${id}`}>{label}<textarea id={`brief-${id}`} data-brief={label} rows="2" placeholder={placeholder}></textarea></label>)}</div>
<div class="actions"><button data-copy-brief>Copy my brief</button><button class="secondary" data-export>Download my workshop notes</button></div><p class="status" data-brief-status role="status"></p><p class="source-note">Notes are held in memory only. Refreshing or closing clears them. Export first if you wish to keep them.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="slide" data-title="29–40 · WorkBuddy build" aria-hidden="true"><div class="slide-inner">
<header class="slide-header"><span class="eyebrow">29–40 min · Build in pairs</span></header>
<div data-language="en"><h2>Brief → one HTML → browser</h2></div><div data-language="zh" lang="zh-Hant" hidden><h2>簡報 → 一個HTML → 瀏覽器</h2></div>
<ol><li><strong>29–31:</strong> Create a new empty workshop folder. Paste your brief with prompt 1; review the alignment.</li><li><strong>31–37:</strong> Use prompt 2 to build one <code>index.html</code>. Keep the scope to one goal and two criteria.</li><li><strong>37–40:</strong> Open the HTML in preview or a browser. Try it before asking a partner to test.</li></ol>
<details><summary>Copy the build prompt</summary><pre id="build-prompt">{prompts[1].text}</pre><button data-copy="build-prompt">Copy build prompt</button><p class="status" role="status"></p></details>
<div class="two"><article><h3>If generation stalls</h3><p>Use the offline lab. Mark on paper where you would change its goal, examples, hints and recheck. Your output is an annotated design and test plan.</p><a href={`${resources}participant-pack.html`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open printable paper pack</a></article><article><h3>Permission boundary</h3><p>Allow only the isolated workshop folder. Review each requested action. Do not grant full access, add an API key, connect a database or upload learner work.</p></article></div><p class="takeaway">Teach the workflow, not identical buttons. WorkBuddy’s files and preview panel may differ from Codex. The test is the activity learners actually use.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="slide" data-title="40–48 · Peer test" aria-hidden="true"><div class="slide-inner">
<header class="slide-header"><span class="eyebrow">40–48 min · Swap roles</span></header>
<div data-language="en"><h2>Try to reveal a design problem</h2><p>Test a strong answer, a weak answer, then a blank response. Record what actually happens; a pleasing screen is not evidence of learning.</p></div><div data-language="zh" lang="zh-Hant" hidden><h2>嘗試揭示設計問題</h2><p>依次測試充分、薄弱及空白作答。記錄實際結果；畫面美觀本身並不代表學習證據。</p></div>
<ul class="checks">{['Strong: can the learner show both criteria and explain the improvement?','Weak: does a relevant hint invite action without inventing a grade?','Blank/whitespace: does feedback wait for an attempt?','Long or unusual text: does the tool preserve it without breaking the layout?','Evidence quality: could a wrong answer reflect confusing instructions? Does the task elicit the intended language ability?', 'Decision quality: does feedback address the actual issue, and what evidence warrants stopping?', 'Keyboard/mobile: can I reach, read and operate every control?','Reset/export: is reset explicit, and does print/download contain the actual first and revised text?','Privacy/offline: no response persistence, collection, network requests or runtime AI?'].map((item,i)=><li><label><input type="checkbox" data-check={item} />{item}</label></li>)}</ul>
<label for="test-notes">Observed behaviour and one priority defect / 觀察與優先問題</label><textarea id="test-notes" data-note="Peer test observations" rows="3"></textarea>
<details><summary>3. {prompts[2].title}</summary><pre id="prompt-2">{prompts[2].text}</pre><button data-copy="prompt-2">Copy prompt 3</button><p class="status" role="status"></p></details>
</div></section>
<section class="slide" data-title="48–52 · Evidence to teaching action" aria-hidden="true"><div class="slide-inner">
<header class="slide-header"><span class="eyebrow">48–52 min · Teacher interpretation</span></header>
<div data-language="en"><h2>Same task, different next steps</h2><p>Choose one fictional case. Discuss an interpretation and an alternative before opening a possible action.</p></div><div data-language="zh" lang="zh-Hant" hidden><h2>同一任務，不同下一步</h2><p>選一個虛構個案，先討論初步詮釋及另一種解釋，再展開可能的行動。</p></div>
<div class="two">{cases.map(c=><article class="case"><h3>{c.id}</h3><p>{c.evidence}</p><details><summary>Possible interpretation, action and recheck</summary><p><strong>Hypothesis:</strong> {c.interpretation}</p><p><strong>Action:</strong> {c.action}</p><p><strong>Fresh recheck:</strong> {c.recheck}</p></details></article>)}</div>
<p class="takeaway">These are provisional hypotheses about responses, not ability labels. The teacher retains the decision. Evidence can also confirm a planned teaching choice.</p>
<label for="case-decision">We noticed… We will… We will check again by… / 我們觀察到… 將會… 再檢視…</label><textarea id="case-decision" data-note="Evidence-to-action decision" rows="4"></textarea>
<details><summary>4. {prompts[3].title}</summary><pre id="prompt-3">{prompts[3].text}</pre><button data-copy="prompt-3">Copy prompt 4</button><p class="status" role="status"></p></details>
</div></section>
<section class="slide" data-title="52–54 · Improve and recheck" aria-hidden="true"><div class="slide-inner">
<header class="slide-header"><span class="eyebrow">52–54 min · One targeted repair</span></header>
<div data-language="en"><h2>Change one thing that matters</h2><p>Choose the defect that most affects the evidence or learner action. Ask for a small repair, then repeat the relevant test.</p></div><div data-language="zh" lang="zh-Hant" hidden><h2>改進一項關鍵設計</h2><p>選出最影響證據或學習者行動的問題，要求小範圍修正，再重做相關測試。</p></div>
<ol class="workshop-steps"><li><strong>Observed problem</strong><span>“The hint appears before any attempt.”</span></li><li><strong>Learning consequence</strong><span>“We cannot see the learner’s own starting point.”</span></li><li><strong>Repair and test</strong><span>Gate the hint, then retest blank and attempted responses.</span></li></ol>
<label for="repair">Our repair, test and observed result / 改進、測試與結果</label><textarea id="repair" data-note="Targeted repair and recheck" rows="4"></textarea><p class="takeaway">Keep a teacher verification step: accuracy, alignment, specificity, clarity, fairness and usefulness. AI output is a draft to inspect.</p><p class="source-note">Hattie &amp; Timperley (2007); Shute (2008)</p>
</div></section>
<section class="slide" data-title="54–59 · Exit ticket and resources" aria-hidden="true"><div class="slide-inner">
<header class="slide-header"><span class="eyebrow">54–59 min · Transfer to your classroom</span></header>
<div data-language="en"><h2>Leave with an action and a recheck</h2></div><div data-language="zh" lang="zh-Hant" hidden><h2>帶走行動與再檢視計劃</h2></div>
<p>Show your usable HTML or low-tech design to a partner and identify one test you actually ran. The artefact serves a formative purpose; its appearance alone is not the outcome.</p><label for="exit-understanding">What makes assessment formative? Explain how your design goes beyond informing-only. / 甚麼使評估具有形成性？</label><textarea id="exit-understanding" data-note="Exit: informing versus formative use" rows="2"></textarea><label for="exit-ai">Where could AI help, and what checks justify that use? / AI可在哪裡協助？如何核對？</label><textarea id="exit-ai" data-note="Exit: justified AI help and checks" rows="2" placeholder="Name a possible use and the checks you would actually run."></textarea><label for="exit-evidence">What language need and response evidence will you focus on? / 語言需要與作答證據？</label><textarea id="exit-evidence" data-note="Exit: evidence" rows="2"></textarea><label for="exit-action">What remains the teacher’s or learner’s responsibility, and what will they do? / 人的責任與行動？</label><textarea id="exit-action" data-note="Exit: action" rows="2"></textarea><label for="exit-recheck">One implementation takeaway: what will you try, and how will fresh evidence check its effect? / 實施重點與再檢視？</label><textarea id="exit-recheck" data-note="Exit: fresh recheck" rows="2"></textarea>
<p class="takeaway">Review AI-generated language aims, tasks, interpretations, feedback and next-step suggestions. People choose suitable information, enact purposeful action and check its effect.</p><div class="actions"><button data-export>Download my workshop notes</button><button class="secondary" data-print>Print my notes / Save PDF</button><a class="download" href={`${resources}participant-pack.html`} download>Download printable resource pack</a><a class="download" href={`${resources}evidence-lab.html`} download>Download offline lab</a><button class="secondary" data-jump="14">Sources and tool guides</button></div>
<p class="status" data-export-status role="status"></p><p class="source-note">The pack contains the brief, prompts, checklist, cases and paper fallback. Your notes and the learner lab are separate; export each where you entered them. No responses are submitted. These tools do not collect a class set across devices.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="slide" data-title="59–60 · School of Education invitation" aria-hidden="true"><div class="slide-inner">
<header class="slide-header"><span class="eyebrow">59–60 min · Optional invitation</span></header>
